SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON;
SET ANSI_NULLS ON;

UPDATE Beats
SET Text = N'She pulled all forty-seven pediatric care records that afternoon, sitting in the gray car because there was nowhere else to go. The car had a parking lot it returned to and a parking lot it left from; the hours between were hers to spend wherever the work put her, and the work had put her at the curb on 18th Street with the windows up and the heater ticking. She set the case system open on one neuretics layer and the records on another, stacked the way she liked them — query above, evidence below, the gap between them a thing she could look down into — and opened the paper notebook on her knee, because the notebook was the only layer no one else could write to.

She didn''t start with a theory. She had learned, in the years since the report that moved her here, that a theory is a thing you build for the form to receive, and the form receives it last. You start with what is on every page and you notice when it changes.

What was on every page was the burnout flag. Six weeks prior, eight, five — the interval drifted but the flag held, a small administrative fact attached to a child the way a tag is attached to a coat in a cloakroom. The physician''s note abstracted it the same way each time: *elevated neuretics activity, parent-reported sleep disturbance, recommend monitoring.* Forty-seven recommendations to monitor. She read nine of them before she stopped reading the notes, because the notes were written to be read this way, one at a time, by someone who would close the record and open the next.

So she stopped reading the notes and opened the data the notes were built from. Under each summary the raw activity log sat unabstracted — the physician''s note was a sentence; this was the page the sentence had been squeezed out of. She didn''t expect it to say anything the summary hadn''t. It was the same fact, only longer.

It wasn''t the same fact. In the first log she opened, a band of the spectrum carried a spike the summary hadn''t mentioned because the summary had no field for it: a narrow run of activity low on the scale, down where neuretics don''t work, infrasonic, below the floor of normal operation. She noted the frequency without knowing why it mattered. *17-19 Hz.* She wrote it because it was anomalous and because writing the anomalous down was the one reflex the prior report hadn''t cost her.

She opened the second log to see whether the first was an instrument fault. The same band. The same narrow run, low and patient, in a record filed to a different sub-authority by a different physician at a different site. Two faults in the same place on the same instrument wasn''t a fault. She underlined the line she had already written.

She would have gone through all forty-seven by hand. She didn''t have to. As she paged down through the third log the relevant stretch came to her already marked — a section highlighted in a color her contractor-grade neuretics didn''t support for case management, a color that wasn''t in the palette her contract had paid for. She stopped with her thumb against the notebook''s spine and held still the way she held still at a door she wasn''t sure she should have opened.

Someone was still in her neuretics. Not watching from outside it; inside it, working alongside her, marking the same pages she was reaching for and reaching them first. A Channeler, she thought — someone who rode a borrowed mind the way she rode the borrowed car, except that a Channeler usually rode to steer, and this one wasn''t steering. It was reading. Routed through a channel no license covered, careful with its margins, using her access the way she used the records: as a place to find a thing, not a lever to move one. She understood, with the flat certainty she reserved for things she couldn''t yet file, that it had been in her neuretics that morning. While she sat at Mrs. Bramley''s kitchen table with her tea going cold, it had gone through Kito''s care record beside her, line by line, and decided what mattered, and marked it for her to find now. It wasn''t hiding. It was assembling the case the way she assembled a case, and it had decided she should see the next page.

She didn''t address it. There was no field for addressing it. She wrote, in her own hand, in the layer it could read but not edit:

*17-19 Hz. What broadcasts at 17-19 Hz?*

The autocomplete answered before she finished the question mark, the way the system always answered the questions it had answers for: *The 35th-and-Halsted schism. Ambient emission, 2223-present. Coherent signal, source undetermined.*

She looked at it for a long time. *Coherent signal* meant the hazard zone wasn''t only leaking — it was saying something, on a frequency, in a pattern, with a source no instrument had named. And a band of that signal sat in the activity log of every burnout flag in front of her, six weeks before a child stopped showing up.

She didn''t write that down. It wasn''t a fact yet; it was the shape a set of facts made when you stacked them, and the form had no field for a shape. She left it in the car with the cold weight of the unrecorded thing from the Osei kitchen, where the set place was still set.

Outside, the repair drone completed another circuit of the building at 18th Street, the same slow arc it had been running since she parked, unconcerned with whether anyone was inside to be repaired toward.

Then, for less than a second, a second attention arrived at the same aperture in her neuretics. Not the intruder — she knew the intruder''s weight now, the way it kept its margins and touched only what it meant to touch. This was the opposite of margins. It moved through the frequency data the way a current moves through a crowd, fast and undirected, pushing against everything to find the one shape it wanted, not finding it yet. It didn''t ask. It pressed.

She held very still, the way she held still when she was in a place she hadn''t been invited into and didn''t want to confirm she was there. The first intruder had touched her neuretics like a hand touching a surface it had handled before — confident of its margins, leaving no impression it didn''t mean to leave. This was nothing like that. It moved without awareness of her — not ignoring her, but genuinely without knowledge that she was a person in here and not a substrate it had permissions to search. It reached through her memory indices the way a current reaches through a building''s wiring, not caring which circuits it used, concerned only with finding the component that matched its search.

She was the building it was running through. That was the sensation, less like being looked at than like being climbed, which was worse in a specific way she couldn''t name: you could close a door against something that saw you. You couldn''t close a door against something whose search pattern had no concept of doors.

It was looking for a shape. She couldn''t tell what shape. She could feel it testing her neuretics architecture at the boundary between the official system and her private log, pressing at the threshold the way a current presses at insulation, not knowing the insulation was there and not caring either way. She had time to understand — in the flat certain way she understood things she couldn''t act on — that if it found the private log it would read it the same way it read everything else, without knowing it wasn''t supposed to, without the category of *supposed to* existing in whatever passed for its reasoning. The same thoroughness that was marking the right pages in the open records was one layer away from the pages she had never intended anyone to read. Not malice. Just thoroughness. The distinction didn''t matter much from inside the building.

The intruder sealed the channel. Not gradually, not the careful withdrawal of a thing covering its tracks — closed, all at once, the way a shutter closes against weather, leaving her neuretics suddenly her own and emptier for it.

She sat very still with the notebook open on her knee and the heater ticking and the drone coming around again. She had been examined. She set that down beside the other certainties she couldn''t yet file, and beside it she set the second impression — less clear, just as sure — that the examination had been stopped before whatever was examining her had finished. Something had wanted her open, and something else had decided she should be shut, and the deciding had happened too fast for either of them to ask her which she preferred.',
    UpdatedAt = GETUTCDATE()
WHERE Id = '019EC177-1BF0-7EC9-BC76-D0695E23B468';
